t the flood of  people leaving their homes is happening  so fast it's creating huge challenges  jamie morocco reports  grabbing her first meal after crossing  the polish ukrainian border katarina  jerichova looks almost relieved i took  money and my documents  and  and it's all maybe one  t-shirt the 25 year old is safe and has  plans to go to a small city outside of  warsaw but for thousands of others who  don't have set plans there are hurdles  they will face if you have a large  number of people  migrating in a short period of time  they're at risk of  infectious disease right now covet is a  big one  as the war began coveted cases in  ukraine were up 550 percent according to  a un report vaccination rates are fairly  low at just 36 percent covet is not  their main priority right now obviously  they're a lot survival and taking care  of their kids and making sure that they  get a next meal as they flee is going to  be their main problem paul spiegel is  the director of the center for  humanitarian health at johns hopkins on  wednesday the canadian will be flying to  poland to support the world health  organization's refugee emergency  response you do everything from tracking  the actual  outbreak of surveillance to then  treatment and it's labor intensive and  that will be  much more challenging in ukraine proper  than outside  forced into tight quarters basements and  bomb shelters covid tuberculosis measles  chickenpox and even polio could threaten  lives experts say shelling has damaged  water systems and communicable diseases  are now also becoming a concern  for those who do get sick hospitals are  already overwhelmed with traumas and  access to essential medicine has  dwindled although a humanitarian  corridor has opened the need is  outpacing supply  for this 19 year old who escaped the  shock of leaving home is setting in  nothing can be important is in my family  the responsibilities host countries now  have  is keeping them healthy and safe jamie  morocco global news toronto  compelled to act a canadian's courage  and kindness  [Music]  what's unfolding in ukraine is driving  thousands of people to ask what they can  do to help for one calgary father who is  visiting family in a country near  ukraine the answer was simple jamie dahl  has his story  it is impossible to measure heartache  but we can all feel it  and in witnessing moments like these a  father forced to say goodbye to his  little girl and wife not knowing if  they'll ever see or hold each other  again  it's hard to detach from the pain being  felt in ukraine i'm emotional now just  saying thinking about that but i know  what that would feel like and  no parent should have to go through that  but dan koska needed to do more than  just bear witness he's a father of two  from calgary he and his family were  neighboring slovakia visiting loved ones  when war broke out there was no question  in my mind i was so determined to  to get to the border and help some  people loaded up with supplies and food  he drove 500 kilometers from the  slovakian capital to the small stretch  of ukrainian border where the u.n  reports more than 90 000 people have  crossed already seeking safety they were  woken up at six a.m on the morning of  the invasion by a bombing of the airport  near their town two days after he got to  the border kotsko was back on the  highway with these two women and their  children who had survived a harrowing  escape from ukraine leaving their  husbands behind they're exhausted  relieved uh several times they they  referred to me they said you're our hero  and  it was very touching to hear that and i  just smiled and accepted it and at the  same time i thought i'm not doing  anything very brave i'm doing something  compassionate but not brave the brave  people are the people they left behind  the men  who are fighting  this war we caught up with the women now  safe in slovakia's capital some slovak  people give us a flat  free free no without any payment we are  brave  and all our citizens are very patriotic  we hope and we believe  that ukraine will win and i think it  will be not  been just  for ukraine it will be been from the for  all well koska is heading back to the  border this weekend with more supplies  and space in his car you need good  people to help other good people  because we're so ugly  war is indeed ugly  thankfully there are many working so  hard to show compassion  amidst the turmoil  jamie dahl global news calgary  and we leave you tonight with another  war going on the fight to control  information in russia the kremlin  silences political opponents and  controls the state media today it went a  step further making it a criminal act to  stray from the kremlin narrative about  this war the duma the russian parliament  unanimously approved a draft law making  it a criminal offense to call what's  happening in ukraine a war or an  invasion anyone who says otherwise and  is convicted faces up to 15 years in  jail in response the bbc's interim  director tweeted this it's with a heavy  heart that we have to suspend bbc news  operations in russia until we assess the  impact of new laws which outlaw  independent journalism multiple news  organizations have followed suit and in  russia multiple sites are blocked  including bbc voice of america deutsche  bella facebook instagram and twitter for  what the kremlin calls discrimination  against russian media  russia's tv reign held its final  broadcast last night  no to war were the final words staff  walked off the set leaving this  [Music]  the russian ballet swan lake  it's what aired on russian state tv in  august of 1991 after the failed coup  attempt that marked the beginning of the  end of the soviet union  thanks for watching  [Music]  you
 

